---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout title="Learning Method" description="The American English Method: observe, understand, describe, speak, practise, receive feedback, reformulate naturally, recall, and transfer.">
  <section class="section method-page">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="back-link" href="/american-english-method/">← Home</a>
      <div class="eyebrow">The method behind the course</div>
      <h1 class="page-title">From understanding to natural speech.</h1>
      <p class="page-lead">American English Method is built around a practical idea: learners need more than vocabulary and rules. They need to notice how language works, understand the situation, find their own words, and use them naturally.</p>

      <section class="method-stage-section" aria-labelledby="learning-sequence">
        <div class="eyebrow">The learning sequence</div>
        <h2 id="learning-sequence">See → Understand → Describe → Speak</h2>
        <div class="method-stage-grid">
          <article class="method-stage"><span>01</span><h3>See</h3><p>Observe a real object, place, situation, image, or conversation. Notice details before reaching for words.</p></article>
          <article class="method-stage"><span>02</span><h3>Understand</h3><p>Connect words and expressions to meaning, context, cultural logic, and the speaker’s intention.</p></article>
          <article class="method-stage"><span>03</span><h3>Describe</h3><p>Build your own sentences. Move from recognising useful language to expressing what you actually notice and mean.</p></article>
          <article class="method-stage"><span>04</span><h3>Speak</h3><p>Use the language in a realistic exchange. Practise choosing a natural response rather than repeating a script mechanically.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="method-stage-section" aria-labelledby="practice-cycle">
        <div class="eyebrow">The practice and feedback cycle</div>
        <h2 id="practice-cycle">Practice → Feedback → Natural Reformulation → Recall → Transfer</h2>
        <ol class="method-cycle">
          <li><strong>Practice</strong><p>Try the task independently: describe, respond, explain, or take part in a role-play.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Feedback</strong><p>Identify what is correct, what needs adjustment, and why. Feedback should explain meaning and usage, not merely mark an answer wrong.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Natural Reformulation</strong><p>Compare your attempt with a natural contemporary American way to express the same idea. Keep your meaning; improve the wording.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Recall</strong><p>Say or write the improved version again without looking. Retrieval helps make the language available when you need it.</p></li>
          <li><strong>Transfer</strong><p>Use the same pattern in a new situation. The goal is flexible communication, not memorisation of one dialogue.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="method-stage-section" aria-labelledby="language-principles">
        <div class="eyebrow">Language principles</div>
        <h2 id="language-principles">Nationwide first. Regional language in context.</h2>
        <div class="method-principle-grid">
          <article class="method-principle"><span class="method-label nationwide">CORE LANGUAGE</span><h3>Nationwide American English</h3><p>The foundation is contemporary, widely understood American English. We prioritise common, natural expressions that are useful across the United States.</p></article>
          <article class="method-principle"><span class="method-label southern">REGIONAL AWARENESS</span><h3>Southern English</h3><p>Southern expressions and speech features are presented as a regional layer. We explain where they are used and when they sound natural; they never replace the nationwide foundation.</p></article>
          <article class="method-principle"><span class="method-label speech">REAL-LIFE LISTENING</span><h3>Connected Speech</h3><p>We explore linking, reductions, rhythm, and other features of fluent speech when they help learners understand what they hear and speak more naturally.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">A regional expression or pronunciation feature is included only when it genuinely belongs in the situation. We do not add Southern English or connected-speech notes simply to fill a template.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="method-stage-section" aria-labelledby="lesson-design">
        <div class="eyebrow">How lessons are designed</div>
        <h2 id="lesson-design">One method. Different lesson formats.</h2>
        <p class="method-intro">The method stays consistent, but the lesson structure adapts to what the learner is doing.</p>
        <div class="method-format-list">
          <div><strong>Real-life scenarios</strong><p>Understand the situation, follow a natural dialogue, notice useful expressions, and practise the interaction.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Descriptive lessons</strong><p>Observe an image or object, learn precise vocabulary, organise details, and describe it independently.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Pronunciation lessons</strong><p>Notice sound patterns, recognise connected speech, practise listening, and reproduce natural rhythm.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Idioms and expressions</strong><p>Learn common, useful phrases with their meaning, register, frequency, and context. Rare or regional items are clearly labelled.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Cultural and regional lessons</strong><p>Understand who uses a form, where it is heard, what it communicates, and when another expression is more appropriate.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="method-stage-section method-final" aria-labelledby="guiding-principle">
        <div class="eyebrow">Our guiding principle</div>
        <h2 id="guiding-principle">Learn the language. Feel the culture. Speak naturally.</h2>
        <p>We do not collect impressive phrases for their own sake. We build language awareness: the ability to understand real speech, choose an appropriate expression, and communicate with confidence in your own voice.</p>
        <a class="method-cta" href="/american-english-method/learn/everyday-scenarios/">Explore the learning directions →</a>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
